course: dictionary
lesson: 1035
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives boot four different meanings: a shoe, the back of a car, a hard kick, and starting a computer. To find the right one, read the words around it, which are its context. «Breaking new boots in a little at a time, and changing wet socks at half-time, makes blisters less likely.» Socks and blisters tell you that boots here are shoes you wear on your feet.
explain: «Kai earned his gift card himself, selling old toys at a car boot sale.» A car boot sale is a market where people sell old things from the back of their cars. So boot here means the space at the back of a car. Always check: does your meaning fit the sentence? A shoe would not make sense here.
fact: Boot can be a noun (a boot, boots) or a verb (to boot, booting).
fact: As a noun, a boot is a strong shoe that covers your foot and ankle, and sometimes part of your leg.
fact: In British and Australian English, the boot of a car is the space at the back for luggage. In the USA it is called the trunk.
fact: To boot something means to kick it hard. To boot up a computer means to start it.
fact: «Get the boot» means to be sent away, and «to boot» means as well.
exercise: «Breaking new boots in a little at a time, and changing wet socks at half-time, makes blisters less likely.» What is a boot in this sentence? | A kick, hard and fast | *A strong shoe that covers your foot and ankle | A computer starting up | The space at the back of a car
exercise: «Kai earned his gift card himself, selling old toys at a car boot sale.» What does boot mean in car boot sale? | To kick something hard | To start up | A strong shoe for walking | *The space at the back of a car for luggage
exercise: Ruby says «boot up» means to kick a computer. What does boot up really mean? | *To start a computer | To wear new shoes | To make someone leave | To kick a computer hard
test: In «She got the boot from the team after breaking the rules», what does boot mean? | *To be made to leave | A winter shoe for snow | To start up | To kick the ball hard
test: Which sentence uses boot to mean the back of a car? | The computer will boot in a minute | *Dad put the shopping in the boot | Mum wore her boots in the snow | He booted the ball over the fence
exam: In «the Man sets out his two boots, his little stone axe, a piece of wood and a hatchet», what are the boots? | *Strong shoes | Tools for chopping wood | The space at the back of a car | A kind of dance
